h vs. $78K for Coleman)", "Expert ratings cluster at Likely/Safe D, with markets pricing her win odds around 94-97%", "District leans Democratic by about two points on the PVI", "No public general-election polling, adding uncertainty"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point clearly to Sykes: a modestly Democratic district, incumbency, a roughly 25-to-1 cash advantage, and expert raters unanimously placing her at Likely D or better, while markets price her near 95%. With no public polling, the margin is trimmed slightly from the fundamentals-based estimate but stays comfortably in Safe D territory.", "watch": "A late surge of outside spending against Sykes or a strong national Republican environment could pull the race back toward the Lean D range.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Emilia Sykes (D) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term incumbent Emilia Sykes seeks re-election in a Northeast Ohio district that leans Democratic by about two points. She faces Republican Carey Coleman, who won a contested May primary, and independent Sandeep Dixit, whose campaign could draw a small share of votes. Sykes has raised over $3.1 million and holds roughly $1.9 million on hand, dwarfing Coleman's war chest, and no significant outside spending has materialized on either side.\n\nEvery major expert rating places the race at Likely D or stronger, and prediction markets give Sykes win odds in the mid-90s. Headlines show Coleman drawing negative local coverage while Sykes maintains a steady district presence. The main caveat is the absence of public general-election polling, so the forecast leans on fundamentals, ratings, and markets, all of which point the same direction.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9765, "margin": 13.33}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9641, "margin": 12.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9651, "margin": 12.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_d